The '''2014 FA Cup final''' was an [[association football]] match between [[Premier League]] clubs [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] and [[Hull City A.F.C.|Hull City]] at [[Wembley Stadium]] in [[London]], England, on 17 May 2014. It was the 133rd FA Cup final overall and the showpiece match of English football's primary cup competition, the Football Association Challenge Cup ([[FA Cup]]), organised by [[the Football Association]] (FA). Hull made their first appearance in an FA Cup Final, while Arsenal equalled [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]]'s record of 18 final appearances.

Each club needed to win five matches to reach the final. Arsenal beat three of their divisional rivals and needed to win a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] to defeat cup holders [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]]. In contrast, four of Hull's opponents were from the lower divisions; they played one [[Replay (sports)|replay]] in the fifth round against [[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton & Hove Albion]].

The final was [[Referee (association football)|refereed]] by [[Lee Probert]] in front of 89,345 spectators. Hull scored two goals in the opening ten minutes from [[James Chester]] and [[Curtis Davies]], but Arsenal came back with goals from [[Santi Cazorla]] and [[Laurent Koscielny]] to level the match by the end of normal time, necessitating [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]]. [[Aaron Ramsey]] scored the winner 11 minutes from the end of the additional period. The victory secured a joint-record 11th victory in the competition for Arsenal. Because Arsenal had already qualified for the [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League]] via their league position, Hull qualified for the [[2014–15 UEFA Europa League]], entering at the third qualifying round.

==Background==
The [[FA Cup]] is an annual [[knockout tournament]] involving professional and amateur men's [[association football|football]] clubs in the [[English football league system]].{{sfn|O'Leary|2017|p=63}} It is the world's oldest football competition.<ref name=finals>{{Cite web |url=http://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/more/finals |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |title=FA Cup Finals, 1872 – today |access-date=9 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170613182709/http://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/more/finals |archive-date=13 June 2017 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="ArsenalManUtdChelseaLiverpool">{{Cite web |url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/who-has-won-most-fa-cups-12-most-successful-clubs-arsenal-man-utd-chelsea-liverpool |work=[[FourFourTwo]] |access-date=5 October 2020 |title=Who has won the most FA Cups? The 12 most successful clubs in the world's oldest competition |first=Sam |last=Blitz |date=3 August 2020 |archive-date=5 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201005103153/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/who-has-won-most-fa-cups-12-most-successful-clubs-arsenal-man-utd-chelsea-liverpool |url-status=live }}</ref> The 2014 final was the 133rd to be played since it was first held in 1872.<ref name=finals/>

[[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] were making their 18th appearance in an [[FA Cup Final]], equalling the record set by [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]]. They had last played in the FA Cup final in [[2005 FA Cup final|2005]], when they defeated Manchester United 5–4 in a [[penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] after the match finished goalless. Conversely, this was [[Hull City A.F.C.|Hull City's]] first appearance in an FA Cup Final since the club was founded in 1904.<ref name=facuplist>{{cite web |title=Cup Final Results |url=http://www.thefa.com/Competitions/FACompetitions/TheFACup/History/cupfinalresults |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |access-date=15 May 2014 |archive-date=10 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140710171239/http://www.thefa.com/Competitions/FACompetitions/TheFACup/History/cupfinalresults |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name=rsssf>{{cite web|title=England FA Challenge Cup Finals |first=James M. |last=Ross |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/engcuphist.html|url-status=live|access-date=23 April 2016 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160512091912/http://www.rsssf.com/tablese/engcuphist.html |archive-date=12 May 2016}}</ref> Arsenal's top scorer during the season was [[Olivier Giroud]] with 16 goals in the league and 6 in other competitions, followed by [[Aaron Ramsey]] with 10 in the league and 6 others.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=142&teamTabs=stats&season_id=143 |title=Arsenal squad details – 2013/2014 – Player appearances |website=Soccerbase |publisher=Centurycomm |access-date=17 June 2021 |archive-date=18 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210618101308/https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=142&teamTabs=stats&season_id=143 |url-status=live }}</ref> Hull's leading scorer was [[Matty Fryatt]] with 6 goals, 4 of which had come in the FA Cup.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=1310&teamTabs=stats&season_id=143 |title=Hull squad details – 2013/2014 – Player appearances |website=Soccerbase |publisher=Centurycomm |access-date=17 June 2021 |archive-date=3 December 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191203014033/https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=1310&teamTabs=stats&season_id=143 |url-status=live }}</ref> Arsenal won both of the league matches between the sides during the 2013–14 season. The fixture at Arsenal's home ground, the [[Emirates Stadium]] in London, in November 2013 ended 2–0, while the return game at the [[KC Stadium]] in Hull the following April saw Arsenal record a 3–0 victory.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/arsenal/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Hull%20City/ |website=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=17 June 2021 |title=Arsenal football club: record v Hull City |archive-date=10 February 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170210214445/http://www.11v11.com/teams/arsenal/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Hull%20City/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

