As a [[Premier League]] club, [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] entered the competition in the third round, where they faced their [[North London derby|north London rivals]], [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]], at the Emirates Stadium. [[Santi Cazorla]] opened the scoring for Arsenal in the 31st minute when he struck [[Serge Gnabry]]'s pass first time past [[Hugo Lloris]], the Tottenham [[Goalkeeper (association football)|goalkeeper]]. Midway through the second half, Tottenham's [[Danny Rose (footballer, born 1990)|Danny Rose]] hesitated in possession, allowing [[Tomáš Rosický]] to win the ball and lift it over Lloris to double Arsenal's lead.<ref name=r3a/> With less than 10 minutes remaining, Arsenal's [[Theo Walcott]] was taken off the pitch injured on a stretcher; he was later ruled out for more than six months with a ruptured [[anterior cruciate ligament]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Theo Walcott: Arsenal forward out of World Cup with knee injury |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25631561 |website=BBC Sport |date=6 January 2014 |access-date=13 April 2014 |archive-date=12 August 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150812061125/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/25631561 |url-status=live }}</ref> The match ended 2–0 and Arsenal progressed to the fourth round.<ref name=r3a>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Arsenal 2–0 Tottenham Hotspur |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25531860 |website=BBC Sport |authorlink=Phil McNulty |date=4 January 2014 |access-date=13 April 2014 |archive-date=9 March 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140309135642/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/25531860 |url-status=live }}</ref> There, they were drawn at home again, this time against [[Coventry City F.C.|Coventry City]] of [[EFL League One|League One]]. [[Lukas Podolski]] gave Arsenal a 2–0 lead within the first half-hour, scoring from [[Mesut Özil]]'s pass before doubling his tally by [[Header (association football)|heading]] in a pass from [[Per Mertesacker]]. Giroud and Cazorla both scored close-range goals in the last 10 minutes of the second half, to give Arsenal a 4–0 victory.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25771717 |first=Emlyn |last=Begley |access-date=16 June 2021 |date=24 January 2014 |website=BBC Sport |title=Arsenal 4–0 Coventry City |archive-date=24 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210524153916/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25771717 |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:Podolski scores, 2014 FA Cup 4th rd, Arsenal vs Coventry.jpg|thumb|left|alt=Arsenal players celebrating Lukas Podolski's goal against Coventry City|Arsenal players celebrating [[Lukas Podolski]]'s goal against Coventry City]]
For the fifth round, Arsenal were drawn against [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] at home, against whom they had lost 5–1 in the Premier League the previous week. [[Alex Oxlade-Chamberlain]] opened the scoring in the 16th minute from inside the Liverpool [[penalty area]] after [[Yaya Sanogo]]'s shot was blocked by [[Steven Gerrard]]. Podolski doubled their lead two minutes after half-time from {{convert|15|yd}}, when he converted a [[Cross (association football)|cross]] from Oxlade-Chamberlain. In the 59th minute, Podolski fouled [[Luis Suárez (Uruguayan footballer)|Luis Suárez]] and Gerrard scored the resulting [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]]. Despite dominating the closing stages of the match, Liverpool failed to score again and the match ended 2–1.<ref>{{cite web |first=Jonathan |last=Jurejko |title=Arsenal 2–1 Liverpool |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/26106019 |website=BBC Sport |date=16 February 2014 |access-date=13 April 2014 |archive-date=13 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140413223934/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/26106019 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the sixth round, Arsenal were again at home where they faced [[Everton F.C.|Everton]]. Özil scored from a Cazorla pass to give Arsenal the lead in the 7th minute, but Everton equalised through [[Romelu Lukaku]] from close range in the 32nd. Midway through the second half, Oxlade-Chamberlain was fouled by [[Gareth Barry]] to concede a penalty; [[Mikel Arteta]] scored on the second attempt after his first successful strike was ruled out as Giroud was deemed to have encroached on the penalty area. Giroud then scored twice in three minutes late on to secure a 4–1 win for Arsenal.<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Arsenal 4–1 Everton |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/26395193 |website=BBC Sport |date=8 March 2014 |access-date=13 April 2014 |archive-date=13 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140413223849/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/26395193 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In their semi-final at [[Wembley Stadium]], which was a [[neutral venue]], Arsenal were drawn against the [[2013 FA Cup final|defending FA Cup holders]], Championship side [[Wigan Athletic F.C.|Wigan Athletic]]. After a goalless first half, Wigan took the lead after [[Callum McManaman]] was brought down in the Arsenal penalty area by Mertesacker and [[Jordi Gómez]] converted the subsequent penalty. With eight minutes of the match remaining, Mertesacker scored with a header from a mishit shot by [[Kieran Gibbs]]. Regular time ended with the scores level at 1–1, and with no goals in [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]], the match went to a penalty shoot-out. [[Łukasz Fabiański]] saved Wigan's first two penalties from [[Gary Caldwell]] and [[Jack Collison]] and as all subsequent strikes were scored, Arsenal won 4–2 and proceeded to the final.<ref>{{cite web |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Wigan Athletic 1–1 Arsenal (aet, 2–4 on pens) |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/26897266 |website=BBC Sport |date=12 April 2014 |access-date=13 April 2014 |archive-date=16 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140416015609/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/26897266 |url-status=live }}</ref> In doing so, Arsenal matched their achievement from the [[1949–50 FA Cup]], when they reached the final without leaving London.<ref name=book>{{cite book |first=John |last=Motson |authorlink=John Motson |title=Motson's FA Cup Odyssey: The World's Greatest Knockout Competition |publisher=Robson |year=2005 |isbn=978-1-86105-903-1 |page=[https://archive.org/details/motsonsfacupodys0000mots/page/139 139] |url=https://archive.org/details/motsonsfacupodys0000mots/page/139 }}</ref>

