Hull City also entered the 2013–14 FA Cup in the third round, where they were drawn away to [[Middlesbrough F.C.|Middlesbrough]]. In the 10th minute, [[Aaron McLean]] put Hull ahead when he struck [[David Meyler]]'s deflected shot past Middlesbrough goalkeeper [[Dimitrios Konstantopoulos]]. Midway through the second half, [[Nick Proschwitz]] scored from inside the Middlesbrough penalty area to give Hull a 2–0 victory.<ref>{{cite web |title=Middlesbrough 0–2 Hull City |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25531662 |website=BBC Sport |date=4 January 2014 |access-date=13 April 2014 |archive-date=9 March 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140309135535/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/25531662 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the fourth round, Hull's opposition were [[EFL League Two|League Two]] side [[Southend United F.C.|Southend United]], whom they faced at [[Roots Hall]]. The first half ended 0–0 and midway through the second, Fryatt scored after receiving a pass from Meyler. Fryatt doubled his and his side's tally in [[stoppage time]], beating [[Dan Bentley (footballer)|Dan Bentley]] in the Southend goal with his strike and securing a 2–0 win for Hull.<ref>{{cite web |title=Southend United 0–2 Hull City |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25789061 |website=BBC Sport |date=25 January 2014 |access-date=13 April 2014 |archive-date=9 March 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140309135101/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/25789061 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In the next round, Hull were drawn against [[EFL Championship|Championship]] team [[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton & Hove Albion]] away at [[Falmer Stadium]]. [[Forward (association football)|Striker]] [[Leonardo Ulloa]] gave Brighton a first-half lead, scoring in the 30th minute after receiving a pass from [[Will Buckley (footballer)|Will Buckley]]. With four minutes of the match remaining, Hull's [[Yannick Sagbo]] levelled the score when he struck [[Sone Aluko]]'s cross through the legs of [[Peter Brezovan]], the Brighton goalkeeper. The match ended 1–1 and a [[Replay (sports)|replay]] was then required at the KC Stadium in Hull to determine the winner of the [[Glossary of association football terms#C|tie]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.thefa.com/news/2014/feb/17/brighton-and-hove-albion-hull-city-report-170214 |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |access-date=17 June 2021 |title=FA Cup Fifth Round report: Brighton & HA 1–1 Hull City |date=17 February 2014 |archive-date=18 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210618101316/https://www.thefa.com/news/2014/feb/17/brighton-and-hove-albion-hull-city-report-170214 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Curtis Davies]] gave Hull the lead after 14 minutes with a header that beat defender [[Jake Forster-Caskey]] on the goal line, before a deflected [[Free kick (association football)|free kick]] from [[Robert Koren]] made it 2–0 before half-time. Ulloa halved the deficit with a header midway through the second half, but the match ended 2–1 to Hull.<ref>{{cite web |first=Emlyn |last=Begley |title=Hull City 2–1 Brighton & Hove Albion |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/26236715 |website=BBC Sport |date=24 February 2014 |access-date=13 April 2014 |archive-date=13 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140413223857/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/26236715 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Hull's opponents in the sixth round were [[Sunderland A.F.C.|Sunderland]] at home. After a goalless first half, Hull scored three times in the space of nine minutes to secure a 3–0 victory. Davies scored with a header in the 68th minute before Meyler dispossessed [[Lee Cattermole]] and made it 2–0 four minutes later. A further mistake from Cattermole allowed Fryatt to score from {{convert|12|yd}} in the 77th minute.<ref>{{cite web |first=Luke |last=Reddy |title=Hull City 3–0 Sunderland |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/26404261 |website=BBC Sport |date=9 March 2014 |access-date=13 April 2014 |archive-date=16 March 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140316042618/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/26404261 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the semi-final, Hull's first since 1930, they faced [[Sheffield United F.C.|Sheffield United]], also at Wembley. [[Jose Baxter]] gave Sheffield United the lead in the 19th minute before Sagbo equalised three minutes before half-time. Two minutes later, [[Stefan Scougall]] scored from [[Jamie Murphy (footballer, born 1989)|Jamie Murphy]]'s cross to make it 2–1 at half-time. Second-half goals from Fryatt, [[Tom Huddlestone]] and [[Stephen Quinn]] gave Hull a 4–2 lead before Murphy scored in the 90th minute to reduce Sheffield United's deficit. Three minutes into stoppage time, Meyler added Hull's fifth and ensured his side a 5–3 victory and progression to the final for the first time in their history.<ref>{{cite web |title=Hull City 5–3 Sheffield United |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/26908665 |website=BBC Sport |date=13 April 2014 |access-date=13 April 2014 |first=Gary |last=Rose |archive-date=13 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140413021331/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/26908665 |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Match==
===Pre-match===
[[File:FA Cup Final 2014 Wembley stadium.jpg|thumb|alt=Music and pyrotechnics|Musical and pyrotechnic performance before the match]]
The [[Referee (association football)|referee]] for the final was [[Lee Probert]], who had previously officiated the [[2010 FA Trophy Final]] and was the [[fourth official]] for the [[2011 FA Cup Final]]. He was [[Assistant referee (association football)|assisted]] by Jake Collin and Mick McDonough, while [[Kevin Friend]] was the fourth official and Simon Bennett was the reserve assistant referee.<ref name=referee>{{Cite web |url=https://www.thefa.com/news/2014/apr/16/final-referee-lee-probert |title=Lee Probert to take charge of the FA Cup Final at Wembley |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |access-date=17 June 2021 |date=16 April 2014 |archive-date=20 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190620025517/http://www.thefa.com/news/2014/apr/16/final-referee-lee-probert |url-status=live }}</ref> Arsenal wore their traditional red-and-white home [[Kit (association football)|kit]] for the final and used the home team dressing room, while their fans were allocated the west end of the stadium. Hull fans occupied the east end and the team played in their amber-and-black home strip.<ref>{{cite web |title=Information for fans going to Wembley |url=http://www.arsenal.com/news/news-archive/20140513/information-for-fans-going-to-wembley |publisher=Arsenal FC |date=13 May 2014 |access-date=15 May 2014 |archive-date=15 May 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140515164958/http://www.arsenal.com/news/news-archive/20140513/information-for-fans-going-to-wembley |url-status=live }}</ref> Ticket prices for the final started at £45 and were also available at £65, £85 and £115, with a £10 discount for concessions, as ticket prices remained the same as the previous FA Cup final. Both clubs were allocated 25,000 tickets, with approximately 20,000 tickets being distributed to volunteers "through the football family", which included counties, leagues, local clubs and charities.<ref>{{cite web |title=FA Cup Final ticket prices and allocations announced |url=http://www.thefa.com/news/the-fa-cup/2014/apr/fa-cup-final-tickets-140414 |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |date=14 April 2014 |access-date=15 May 2014 |archive-date=29 November 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141129100743/http://www.thefa.com/news/the-fa-cup/2014/apr/fa-cup-final-tickets-140414 |url-status=live }}</ref> The financial prize for winning the FA Cup Final was £1.8&nbsp;million.<ref name=referee/>

The traditional pre-match anthem, "[[Abide with Me]]", and the [[God Save the Queen|national anthem]] were performed by the winner of [[The X Factor (British series 3)|the third series]] of ''[[The X Factor (British TV series)|The X Factor]]'', [[Leona Lewis]], accompanied by the [[Band of the Welsh Guards]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Leona Lewis to sing National Anthem at FA Cup Final |url=http://www.thefa.com/news/2014/may/leona-lewis-to-sing-at-fa-cup-final |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |date=13 May 2014 |access-date=15 May 2014 |archive-date=14 May 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140514213346/http://www.thefa.com/news/2014/may/leona-lewis-to-sing-at-fa-cup-final |url-status=live }}</ref> Arsenal were without long-term injured attackers Walcott and Gnabry, while Oxlade-Chamberlain and captain [[Thomas Vermaelen]] faced late fitness tests.<ref name=selection/> While Vermaelen made the bench, Oxlade-Chamberlain missed the final.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/may/18/arsene-wenger-fa-cup-arsenal-hull-trophy-drought|title=Arséne Wenger savours FA Cup win over Hull as Arsenal end drought|date=18 May 2014|work=Guardian|access-date=20 May 2014|archive-date=8 July 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170708164526/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/may/18/arsene-wenger-fa-cup-arsenal-hull-trophy-drought|url-status=live}}</ref> Hull strikers [[Shane Long]] and [[Nikica Jelavić]] were [[cup-tied]], having appeared earlier in the tournament for [[West Bromwich Albion F.C.|West Bromwich Albion]] and Everton respectively.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/10838021/FA-Cup-final-2014-Arsenal-vs-Hull-City-live.html |title=FA Cup final 2014, Arsenal v Hull City: as it happened |date=18 May 2014 |work=Daily Telegraph |access-date=20 May 2014 |archive-date=19 May 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140519184001/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/10838021/FA-Cup-final-2014-Arsenal-vs-Hull-City-live.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Aluko, [[Paul McShane (footballer)|Paul McShane]], [[James Chester]] and [[Robbie Brady]] faced fitness tests for Hull; only Chester was deemed fit enough to start the match, Aluko and McShane were on the bench, and Brady missed out altogether. Hull welcomed back goalkeeper [[Allan McGregor]] from a kidney injury.<ref name=selection>{{cite news|title=Arsenal v Hull|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27354148|access-date=16 May 2014|website=BBC Sport|date=16 May 2014|archive-date=17 May 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140517025727/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/27354148|url-status=live}}</ref> Arsenal adopted a [[Formation (association football)#4–2–3–1|4–2–3–1 formation]] while Hull lined up as a 3–5–1–1.<ref name=bbcrep>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27354148 |first=Phil |last=McNulty |authorlink=Phil McNulty |access-date=17 June 2021 |date=17 May 2014 |title=Arsenal 3–2 Hull City |website=BBC Sport |archive-date=4 May 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200504112207/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27354148 |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Summary===

====First half====
[[File:FA Cup Final 2014 01.jpg|thumb|alt=Arsenal in the 2014 FA Cup final|Arsenal enjoyed 65% of possession during the course of the match.]]
Hull City [[Kick-off (association football)|kicked off]] the match at around 5&nbsp;p.m. on 17 May 2014 in front of 89,345 spectators. In the 4th minute, a corner from Quinn found Huddlestone on the edge of the Arsenal penalty area and his shot was diverted into the goal by Chester to give Hull a 1–0 lead. Three minutes later, Giroud received treatment for an injury before Davies doubled Hull's lead in the 9th minute. Huddlestone struck a free kick from the right-hand side of the pitch into Arsenal's penalty area that the defenders failed to clear. [[Alex Bruce (footballer, born 1984)|Alex Bruce]] headed the ball goalbound and Fabiański pushed it against the post before Davies struck it into the goal from close range to make it 2–0. In the 14th minute, Hull were close to scoring their third after Bruce's header from a [[set piece (football)|set piece]] was cleared off the Arsenal goal line by Gibbs. Two minutes later, Cazorla was fouled by Bruce and won a free kick. From around {{convert|27|yd}}, Cazorla struck the ball past McGregor and into the Hull net to make it 2–1.<ref name=guardasit>{{Cite web |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/may/17/fa-cup-final-arsenal-hull-city-report |first=Paul |last=Doyle |access-date=17 June 2021 |title=Arsenal win FA Cup final after 3–2 win over Hull City – as it happened |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=17 May 2014 |archive-date=25 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210525030544/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/may/17/fa-cup-final-arsenal-hull-city-report |url-status=live }}</ref>

Midway through the half, Podolski crossed for Özil, whose run had beaten Bruce, but he missed the ball altogether from close range. In the 25th minute, Özil's shot from the edge of the Hull penalty area was headed clear by Davies. Arsenal increased the pressure and Giroud shot off-target in the 28th minute before Ramsey's strike from a corner was also cleared by Davies. With two minutes of the half remaining, Huddlestone's shot from around {{convert|30|yd}} went narrowly over the Arsenal crossbar. After three minutes of stoppage time, the first half was brought to a close with Hull leading 2–1.<ref name=guardasit/>

====Second half====
Neither side made any [[Substitute (association football)|substitutions]] during the interval and Arsenal kicked off the second half. Five minutes in, Özil ran onto a [[Chip (association football)|chipped]] pass forward and sent in a cross, but none of his teammates were in the penalty area. In the 56th minute, the spectators applauded in memory of the 56 victims of the [[Bradford City stadium fire]] of 1985. Two minutes later, Giroud fell in the Hull penalty area under a challenge from Huddlestone, but the referee adjudged it to have been a legitimate tackle and did not award a penalty. In the 60th minute, Huddlestone became the first player of the match to be shown a [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] for a foul. Soon after, Arsenal made their first change; Podolski was substituted for Sanogo and they altered their formation to play with two strikers. Sanogo missed a chance to score from a header in the 64th minute before Hull replaced Bruce with McShane.<ref name=guardasit/>

In the 68th minute, Cazorla was brought down by Davies in the Hull penalty area, but the referee turned down the appeals for a penalty, before Meyler was booked for a foul on [[Laurent Koscielny]]. With 18 minutes of the match remaining, Arsenal equalised to make it 2–2. Sagna won a header from a corner and the ball deflected to Koscielny, who was {{convert|3|yd|0}} from the goal line, where he turned and struck the ball into the net. Three minutes later, Hull made their second substitution, with Aluko coming on for Quinn. In the 79th minute, Arsenal's Gibbs struck a shot over the crossbar from close range. McGregor then saved a shot from Giroud before the Arsenal player was booked for a foul on Aluko. Davies was then shown a yellow card for a foul on Giroud. In the final minute of regular time, Sanogo struck a shot wide of the Hull goal from the edge of the penalty area. In the third of three minutes of stoppage time, Özil passed to Giroud, whose shot was saved by McGregor. The second half ended with the scores level at 2–2, sending the match into extra time.<ref name=guardasit/>

====Extra time====
[[File:1 aaron ramsey 2015 (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|alt=Aaron Ramsey|Aaron Ramsey ''(pictured in 2015)'' scored the winning goal in the second half of extra time.]]
Arsenal dominated the early stages of extra time and had a 93rd-minute shot from Özil blocked by Chester, before Giroud's header struck the Hull crossbar. Midway through the first period of additional time, Ramsey struck a shot from {{convert|30|yd}} that was saved by McGregor. In the 99th minute, Ramsey hit the ball into the [[Glossary of association football terms#S|side netting]] after exchanging passes with Giroud. With three minutes of the first half remaining, Hull were forced to make their final substitution when [[Liam Rosenior]] was injured, and he was replaced by [[George Boyd (footballer)|George Boyd]]. A minute before half-time, Cazorla's curling shot from around {{convert|13|yd}} was off target and the first period of extra time ended with the score still level at 2–2.<ref name=guardasit/>

Before the second half began, Arsenal made a double-substitution, with Rosický and [[Jack Wilshere]] replacing Özil and Cazorla. In the 109th minute, Giroud backheeled the ball to Ramsey, who struck it into the Hull goal from around {{convert|14|yd}} to give Arsenal a 3–2 lead. With six minutes remaining, Sanogo's shot from {{convert|15|yd}} was wide of the goal, before a mistake from Mertesacker allowed Aluko to go around Fabiański, but his shot was also wide. In the 118th minute, Giroud went down in the area, once again appealing for a penalty, but Sanogo continued to play and his shot was saved by McGregor. In the final minute, Fabiański saved a shot from Aluko, and the match ended 3–2, with Arsenal winning the FA Cup.<ref name=guardasit/>

